The DriverPass project was for a client who wanted a system to help students prepare for their driving tests. The client needed a system that would allow students to schedule driving lessons, access online practice tests and lessons, manage their accounts, and track their progress. The system also needed to help driving instructors and office staff manage schedules, student information, and lesson records.

What I Did Well

I think I did a good job identifying the client's needs and organizing them into requirements for the system. I also worked on creating UML diagrams to show how the different users would interact with the system. This helped me understand how to turn the client's ideas into a design that could be used to develop the software.

What I Would Improve

If I could revise one part of my work, I would improve my business requirements document and system design document by making sure every requirement was clearly explained and connected to the system's features. I would also review my diagrams to make sure they included all the necessary details and were easy for someone else to understand. This would make my work more complete and professional.

Interpreting User Needs

I interpreted the client's needs by identifying the main users of the system, including students, instructors, office staff, and administrators. I used those needs to plan features such as scheduling lessons, managing accounts, accessing practice tests, and tracking student progress. Considering the user's needs is important because the system should solve the problems the client is experiencing and be easy for people to use. If the design does not meet the users' needs, the finished system may not work as expected.

My Approach to Software Design

When designing software, I would start by understanding the client's goals and gathering the system requirements. Next, I would identify the users, determine what the system needs to do, and create UML diagrams to show how the system should work. I would also review the design to make sure it meets the requirements before development begins. In the future, I would continue using clear documentation, user feedback, and regular reviews to help create systems that are organized, reliable, and easy to use.
